85 false] [17592186088898 1064 "There are some special types of square matrices.\n\n- A square matrix is called **diagonal**, if all entries that do not lie on the main diagonal (from top-left to bottom-right) are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all diagonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal.\n\n- An **identity matrix** is a diagonal matrix where the entries on the diagonal are all $1$'s (and all other entries are $0$'s). For example, the following matrices are all identity matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\,1\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe use the notation $I_n$ for the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFinally, as we've seen before:\n\n- If a matrix has only one row, then it's called a **row vector**. An example of a row vector is shown below.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- If a matrix has only one column, then it's called a **column vector**. An example of a column vector is shown below.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186088898 1064 "There are some special types of square matrices.\n\n- A square matrix is called **diagonal**, if all entries that do not lie on the main diagonal (from top-left to bottom-right) are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all diagonal matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal.\n- An **identity matrix** is a diagonal matrix where the entries on the diagonal are all $1$'s (and all other entries are $0$'s). For example, the following matrices are all identity matrices:\n$[1]$\nWe use the notation $I_n$ for the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFinally, as we've seen before:\n\n- If a matrix has only one row, then it's called a **row vector**. An example of a row vector is shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- If a matrix has only one column, then it's called a **column vector**. An example of a column vector is shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186088899 1064 "Given the scalar $a$, the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number\n\n$$\n2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nbecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 = 2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A$, the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A$, first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1}$, then we get the identity matrix $I_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A^{-1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\cdot (-2) + (-1) \\cdot 1 & -1 \\cdot (-1) + (-1) \\cdot 1 \\\\ 1 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot 1 & 1 \\cdot (-1) + 2 \\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I_{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A$, then we also get the identity matrix $I_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) &= I_{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186088899 1064 "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186088900 1064 "The number $0$ does not have an inverse for multiplication over the real numbers, because there's no such number $a$ that\n\n$$\na \\cdot 0 = 1\n$$\n\nInstead, for every $a$ we have\n\n$$\na \\cdot 0 = 0\n$$\n\nSimilarly, not every matrix has an inverse.\n\n- If a matrix is not square, an inverse does not exist.\n- Some square matrices do not have an inverse. If a square matrix does not have an inverse, then it is said to be **singular**. An example of a singular matrix is the zero matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe will soon learn how to calculate the inverse of a square matrix, and we'll also soon learn how to determine when a square matrix does not have an inverse." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186088900 1064 "The number $0$ does not have an inverse for multiplication over the real numbers, because there's no such number $a$ that $a \\cdot 0 = 1.$ Instead, for every $a$ we have $a \\cdot 0 = 0.$\n\nSimilarly, not every matrix has an inverse.\n\n- If a matrix is not square, an inverse does not exist.\n\n- Some square matrices do not have an inverse. If a square matrix does not have an inverse, then it is said to be **singular**. An example of a singular matrix is the zero matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe will soon learn how to calculate the inverse of a square matrix, and we'll also soon learn how to determine when a square matrix does not have an inverse." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186088901 1064 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe **inverse** of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186088901 1064 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe **inverse** of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186088902 1064 "Remember that the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $\\det(A)=0,$ then $A^{-1}$ doesn't exist since we can't divide by zero. In fact, we have the following statement:\n\n> $A^{-1}$ exists if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nA matrix that has an inverse is called **invertible**. Otherwise, the matrix doesn't have an inverse and is called **non-invertible**. A square matrix that is non-invertible is called **singular**." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186088902 1064 "Remember that the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $\\det(A)=0$, then $A^{-1}$ doesn't exist since we can't divide by zero. In fact, we have the following statement:\n\n> $A^{-1}$ exists if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nA matrix that has an inverse is called **invertible**. Otherwise, the matrix doesn't have an inverse and is called **non-invertible**. A square matrix that is non-invertible is called **singular**." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186088903 1064 "Given a matrix $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]$\nwe have been using the following formula to compute the inverse:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo check that this formula is correct for a general matrix $A$, we need to verify that\n\n$$\nAA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I_2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $A^{-1}A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}\\cdot A & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}[\\begin{aligned}ad-bc & 0 \\\\ 0 & -bc+ad\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 &