{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} =-j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j'$, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c2dc9f76bb23225cec0a4f62541d8a74c4c4b16d2e2f7c37e8d36922d97c59f0.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation $\\mathbf R$ represented by the matrix $R$ defines a **counterclockwise rotation of $180^\\circ$ (or $\\pi$ radians) about the origin.**\n\nNote that we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation $\\mathbf R$ can also be considered a rotation of $180^\\circ$ (or $\\pi$ radians) *clockwise* about the origin." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186093440 1064 "Finally, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nbe a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf R$. As usual, we can find the images of $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{R}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^{′} &= R(i) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} =-j \\\\\nj^{′} &= R(j) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j'$, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fc5f33d0bd7047ab02bd9bc3ea3b00c083ab214f3bc8a51e9cbb6e54dc6b51b.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation $\\mathbf R$ represented by the matrix $R$ defines a **counterclockwise rotation of $270^\\circ$, or $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf R$ can also be considered a rotation of $90^\\circ$, or\n\n$$\n\\dfrac \\pi 2\n$$\n\nradians *clockwise* about the origin." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186093440 1064 "Finally, let \n\n!