uare $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101968 1064 "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A$.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot 0 \\\\ 1 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A$.\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2$, the result is the second column of $A$.\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 & (-1) \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186101968 1064 "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf we mu