angle 2,-1 \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/10/102f72ba6730d1b07ca0d01186a1ff7851954193f00dc74eb7150981844b0222.png)\n\nNow, we construct a new matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Let's find the determinant of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|det(M)| \\\\  & =|-7| \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101970 1064 "Why does the absolute value of the determinant represent the area of the parallelogram? Let's prove this result.\n\nFirst, let's state the result precisely. Consider two vectors\n\n$$\n\\textbf{u}=\\langle a,b \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textbf{v}=\\langle c,d \\rangle\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/3a/3a466fd3c52ea345d80b9ba67ac104efef29bfd7eeed0e4567364c80f3212c0d.png)\n\nWe construct a new matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Then the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$:\n\n$$\nA = \\mid \\det(M) \\mid\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we use the following picture:\n\n![](images/3d/3da110b114d2038738bd4ef06cb031bb8b66aee062ffc83955dfdaade72584e1.png)\n\nWe can compute the area by starting with the area of the outer rectangle, and then subtracting off the area of the smaller triangles and rectangles surrounding the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\mid (a + c)(b + d) - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}cd- 2 \\cdot ad- 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}ab \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid ab + ad + bc + cd - cd - 2ad - ab \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid bc - ad \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid ad - bc \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid det(M) \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186101970 1064 "Why does the absolute value of the determinant represent the area of the parallelogram? Let's prove this result.\n\nFirst, let's state the result precisely. Consider two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle a,b \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle c,d \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/3a/3a466fd3c52ea345d80b9ba67ac104efef29bfd7eeed0e4567364c80f3212c0d.png)\n\nWe construct a new matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Then the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA = |\\det(M)|\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we use the following picture:\n\n![](images/3d/3da110b114d2038738bd4ef06cb031bb8b66aee062ffc83955dfdaade72584e1.png)\n\nWe can compute the area by starting with the area of the outer rectangle, and then subtracting off the area of the smaller triangles and rectangles surrounding the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|(a+c)(b+d)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}cd-2\\cdot ad-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}ab| \\\\  & =|ab+ad+bc+cd-cd-2ad-ab| \\\\  & =|bc-ad| \\\\  & =|ad-bc| \\\\  & =|det(M)|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101971 1064 "We've been using the fact that\n\n$$\nAI_n = I_n A = A\n$$\n\nfor any $n \\times n$ matrix $A$. But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2$. That is, let's prove that\n\n$$\nAI_2=I_2A = A\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2