begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101972 1064 "When two vectors are parallel, the parallelogram does not exist. So, it has zero area.\n\n![](images/b6/b65c64ccf16764f99c0c30dbf6b37ce7b6254b83496512a5d207dddab095dac7.png)\n\nAs a result, the determinant of a matrix whose columns are parallel vectors must also be zero.\n\nFor example, in the diagram above, we have the vectors\n\n$$\n\\textbf{u} = \\left< 1,1 \\right>\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textbf{v} = \\left< 2,2 \\right>\n$$\n\nConstructing a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the determinant does come out to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(M) &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186101972 1064 "When two vectors are parallel, the parallelogram does not exist. So, it has zero area.\n\n![](images/b6/b65c64ccf16764f99c0c30dbf6b37ce7b6254b83496512a5d207dddab095dac7.png)\n\nAs a result, the determinant of a matrix whose columns are parallel vectors must also be zero. \n\nFor example, in the diagram above, we have the vectors $\\textbf{u} = \\left< 1,1 \\right>$ and $\\textbf{v} = \\left< 2,2 \\right>.$ Constructing a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIndeed, the determinant does come out to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101979 1064 "Given the parametric equations of a curve, we can find its Cartesian equation by eliminating the parameter.\n\nFor instance, consider the parametric curve given by\n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2 \\leq t \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTo find the Cartesian equation of the curve, we start with the $x$ equation and solve for $t$:\n\n$$\nx=2t \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute this into our expression for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= t^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{x}{2})^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3}}{2^{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3}}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3\n$$\n\nHowever, our parametric curve was subject to the constraint\n\n$$\n-2\\leq t \\leq 2\n$$\n\nSince $x=2t$, the corresponding constraint for $x$ is\n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3, \\quad -4 \\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nThe graph of this curve is shown below:\n\n![](images/46/4697114d3ce40272a49dff794c4375301ae346e81b823049f587f8780172c413.png)" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186101979 1064 "Given the parametric equations of a curve, we can find its Cartesian equation by eliminating the parameter.\n\nFor instance, consider the parametric curve given by\n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2 \\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nTo find the Cartesian equation of the curve, we start with the $x$ equation and solve for $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute this into our expression for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3.$\n\nHowever, our parametric curve was subject to the constraint $-2\\leq t \\leq 2.$ Since $x=2t,$ the corresponding constraint for $x$ is $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3, \\quad -4 \\leq x \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nThe graph of this curve is shown below:\n\n![](images/46/4697114d3ce40272a49dff794c4375301ae346e81b823049f587f8780172c413.png)" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101980 1064 "When parametric equations contain trigonometric equations, we can often eliminate the parameter using a trigonometric identity.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos t, \\,\\,\\, y = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sin t, \\quad 0 \\leq t\\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nFor the sine and cosine functions, we have the following Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\, & \\Rightarrow \\,\\operatorname{cos}t=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}t\\, & \\Rightarrow \\,\\operatorname{sin}t=3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+(3y)^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $4x^2 + 9y^2 = 1.$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101980 1064 "When parametric equations contain trigonometric equations, we can often eliminate the parameter using a trigonometric identity.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos t, \\,\\,\\, y = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sin t, \\quad 0 \\leq t\\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nFor the sine and cosine functions, we have the following Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = \\frac{1}{2}\\cos t & ⇒\\cos t = 2x \\\\ y = \\frac{1}{3}\\sin t & ⇒\\sin t = 3y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x)^{2} + (3y)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n4x^{2} + 9y^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\n4x^2 + 9y^2 = 1\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186101982 1064 "The **binomial theorem** states that for any real numbers $a$ and $b$ and any positive integer $n$,\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n$$\n{n \\choose k} = \\dfrac{n!}{k!\\, (n-k)!}\n$$\n\nLet's use the binomial theorem to find the first few terms in the expansion of\n\n$$\n(2+x)^6\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a = 2$, $b= x$, and $n=6$. Therefore, the first three terms of our expansion are given by\n\n$$\n{\\left({2 + x} \\right)^{6}} = {6\\choose0} {\\left(2 \\right)^{6}}{\\left(x \\right)^0} + {6\\choose1} {\\left(2 \\right)^{6 - 1}}{\\left(x \\right)^1} + {6\\choose2} {\\left(2 \\right)^{6 - 2}}{\\left(x \\right)^2} + \\cdots\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n{6\\choose0} = {\\color{blue}{1}}, \\qquad {6\\choose1} = {\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad {6\\choose2} = {\\color{blue}{15}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expansion can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 + x)^{6} &= (\\frac{6}{0})(2)^{6}(x)^{0} + (\\frac{6}{1})(2)^{6 - 1}(x)^{1} + (\\frac{6}{2})(2)^{6 - 2}(x)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= (1)(2^{6})(1) + (6)(2^{5})(x) + (15)(2^{4})(x^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= (1)(64)(1) + (6)(32)(x) + (15)(16)(x^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 64 + 192x + 240x^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186101982 1064 "The **binomial theorem** states that for any real numbers $a$ and $b$ and any positive integer $n,$ \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n},\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that \n\n$$\n{n \\choose k} = \\dfrac{n!}{k!\\, (n-k)!  }.\n$$\n\nLet's use the binomial theorem to find the first few terms in the expansion of\n\n$$\n(2+x)^6.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a = 2,$ $b= x,$ and $n=6.$  Therefore, the first three terms of our expansion are given by\n\n$$\n{\\left( {2 + x} \\right)^{6}}   =  \n{6\\choose0}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6}}{\\left( x \\right)^0} +  {6\\choose1}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6 - 1}}{\\left( x \\right)^1} +  {6\\choose2}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6 - 2}}{\\left( x \\right)^2} + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n{6\\choose0} = {\\color{blue}{1}}, \\qquad {6\\choose1} = {\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad {6\\choose2} = {\\color{blue}{15}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expansion can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{6} & =(\\frac{6}{0}){(2)}^{6}{(x)}^{0}+(\\frac{6}{1}){(2)}^{6-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\frac{6}{2}){(2)}^{6-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =(1)({2}^{6})(1)+(6)({2}^{5})(x)+(15)({2}^{4})({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =(1)(64)(1)+(6)(32)(x)+(15)(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =64+192\\,x+240\\,{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186101991 1064 "We can only multiply two matrices if they are **conformable** for multiplication, which means that the number of columns in the first matrix must equal the number of rows in the second matrix. In other words, we can only multiply two matrices if the first matrix has dimensions $n$ and the second matrix has dimensions $n$\n\nIf the matrices $A$ and $B$ (in that order) are conformable for multiplication and have dimensions $n$ and $n$ respectively, then the product $AB$ will have the dimensions $m \\times k$.\n\nLet's illustrate these ideas with some concrete examples." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186101991 1064 "We can only multiply two matrices if they are **conformable** for multiplication, which means that the number of columns in the first matrix must equal the number of rows in the second matrix. In other words, we can only multiply two matrices if the first matrix has dimensions $n$ and the second matrix has dimensions $n$\n\nIf the matrices $A$ and $B$ (in that order) are conformable for multiplication and have dimensions $n$ and $n$ respectively, then the product $AB$ will have the dimensions $m \\times k.$\n\nLet's illustrate these ideas with some concrete examples." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186102033 1064 "Suppose that we have the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 (x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x$. Can we solve it using the method of integration by substitution?\n\nThe answer is yes, and the procedure is very similar to the one that we followed for the indefinite integrals. The main difference is that this time, we need to change the limits of integration too. We proceed via three steps, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: Change of variable in the integrand.\n\nWe start by setting the base of the power equal to $u$. Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x - 1 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 1 \\\\\ndu &= dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x=u^5\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculate the new limits.\n\nWe now have to change the limits of integration, $x=1$ and $x=2$. Since we want to express the integral in terms of the new variable, $u$, we need to change the variable in the limits too.\n\nThe lower limit $x=1$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x - 1 \\\\\nu &= 1 - 1 \\\\\nu &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile the upper limit $x=2$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x - 1 \\\\\nu &= (2) - 1 \\\\\nu &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is useful to use a table like the following when changing limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Solve the integral in the new variable $u$, using the new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{2}(x - 1)^{5}dx &= \\int_{0}^{1}u^{5}du \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{6}u^{6}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(1)^{6} - \\frac{1}{6}(0)^{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when applying integration by substitution to definite integrals, there is no need to substitute the variable $x$ back in the final answer. We can simply evaluate it using the new variable $u$." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186102033 1064 "Suppose that we have the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 (x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Can we solve it using the method of integration by substitution?\n\nThe answer is yes, and the procedure is very similar to the one that we followed for the indefinite integrals. The main difference is that this time, we need to change the limits of integration too. We proceed via three steps, as follows.\n\n** Step 1**: Change of variable in the integrand. \n\nWe start by setting the base of the power equal to $u.$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x=u^5\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\n** Step 2**: Calculate the new limits. \n\nWe now have to change the limits of integration, $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Since we want to express the integral in terms of the new variable, $u,$ we need to change the variable in the limits too.\n\nThe lower limit $x=1$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =1-1 \\\\ u & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile the upper limit $x=2$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =(2)-1 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is useful to use a table like the following when changing limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Solve the integral in the new variable $u,$ using the new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{6}-\\frac{1}{6}(0)^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when applying integration by substitution to definite integrals, there is no need to substitute the variable $x$ back in the final answer. We can simply evaluate it using the new variable $u.$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186102035 1064 "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x)$. Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x &=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt] & = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2 +C \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}\\ln^{2} x + C) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln^{2} x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}[(\\ln x)^{2}] + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2\\ln x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x) + 0 \\\\\n&= \\ln x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\ln x}{x}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186102035 1064 "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({ln}^{2}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186102036 1064 "Let's evaluate the following definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^4} \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x }\n$$\n\nWe'll use the change of variable method, which comprises three steps:\n\n**Step 1:**  Change the variable.\n\nLet's introduce the new variable $u = \\ln x.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\textrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:**  Find the new limits of integration.\n\nWe use the table below to change our limits of integration from $x$ to $u{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\n**Step 3:** Evaluate the integral in the new variable $u.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6(\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{1}) \\\\  & =6(2-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186102036 1064 "Let's evaluate the following definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^4} \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe'll use the change of variable method, which comprises three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Change the variable.\n\nLet's introduce the new variable $u = \\ln x$. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{x} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\textrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Find the new limits of integration.\n\nWe use the table below to change our limits of integration from $x$ to $u{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & e & e^{4} \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Evaluate the integral in the new variable $u$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{e}^{e^{4}}\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\ln x}}dx &= \\int_{e}^{e^{4}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{x} \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{u}} \\cdot du \\\\\n&= 3\\int_{1}^{4}u^{-1/2}du \\\\\n&= \\left.6u^{1/2}\\right|_{1}^{4} \\\\\n&= 6(\\sqrt{4} - \\sqrt{1}) \\\\\n&= 6(2 - 1) \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186102037 1064 "Suppose we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x}$, the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y$, into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2y \\\\\n2e^{2x}_(⏟)_(y^{′}) &= 2 \\cdot e^{2x}_(⏟)_(y) \\\\\n2e^{2x} &= 2e^{2x}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186102037 1064 "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ {{2{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot {{{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107077 1064 "Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-1,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb5c1121b48fc5c377260030b9ea8028baa637d4ce119dd217fcd989997b0b10.png)\n\nTo compute the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a}$, we can use the Pythagorean theorem in three dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\sqrt{(2)^{2} + (-1)^{2} + (3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 1 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{x} \\\\ a_{y} \\\\ a_{z}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2+a_z^2}\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107077 1064 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-1,3 \\rangle,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb5c1121b48fc5c377260030b9ea8028baa637d4ce119dd217fcd989997b0b10.png)\n\nTo compute the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a},$ we can use the Pythagorean theorem in three dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{x} \\\\ {a}_{y} \\\\ {a}_{z}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2+a_z^2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $|| \\mathbf a ||$ to denote the magnitude of a vector. \n\n\\begin{align*}\n\t|\\overrightarrow{OX}| &= |2\\mathbf{i}| \\\\\n\t\t&= 2 \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{i}\\,| \\\\\n\t\t&= 2; \\\\\n\t|\\overrightarrow{XZ}| &= |-\\mathbf{j}| \\\\\n\t\t&= |-1| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,| \\\\\n\t\t&= 1;\\\\\n\t|\\overrightarrow{ZA}| &= |3\\mathbf{k}| \\\\\n\t\t&= 3 \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{k}\\,| \\\\\n\t\t&= 3.\n\\end{align*}\n\nUsing Pythagorean Theorem, from [math]\\bigtriangleup OXZ[/math] we obtain\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\t|\\overrightarrow{OZ}| &= \\sqrt{|\\overrightarrow{OX}|^2\n\t\t\t+|\t\\overrightarrow{XZ}|^2} \\\\\n\t\t&= \\sqrt{2^2+1^2} \\\\\n\t\t&= \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{align*}\n\nand from [math]\\bigtriangleup OZA[/math]:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\t|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| &= \\sqrt{|\\overrightarrow{OZ}|^2\n\t\t\t+|\t\\overrightarrow{ZA}|^2} \\\\\n\t\t&= \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^2+3^2} \\\\\n\t\t&= \\sqrt{14}.\n\\end{align*}" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107078 1064 "Consider the two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-2, 2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 3,0,-1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nTo find the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors, we can rearrange the dot product formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b\\begin{vmatrix}} \\\\ θ|= \\arccos (\\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b\\end{vmatrix}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y} + a_{z}b_{z} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the magnitudes of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3, \\\\\n\\mid b \\mid &= \\sqrt{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2} + b_{z}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 0^{2} + (-1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\Bigg(\\dfrac{1}{3\\sqrt{10}} \\Bigg) \\approx 83.9^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107078 1064 "Consider the two vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-2, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 3,0,-1 \\rangle$ shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nTo find the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors, we can rearrange the dot product formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot |\\,b\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the magnitudes of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ |\\,b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\Bigg( \\dfrac{1}{3\\sqrt{10}} \\Bigg) \\approx 83.9^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107160 1064 "Consider the triangle below with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,1)$ and $(2,3)$.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c16deb083b467c063c900196274d08c532ad4633354f375530cb2cbe270491.png)\n\nWhat is the image of this triangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represented by the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all vertices of our triangle written as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,0)$. Notice that these points lie on the same line. Two of them even coincide!\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the following line segment:\n\n![](images/88/88383b8eca1805c158f80d998d844d4c666b15d118a0ec26b4a1ba43c781fc76.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ and region $D$, we have\n$\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = \\mid \\det(T) \\mid \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)$.\nSince $\\mid \\det(T) \\mid = 0$ in this case, any object applied to $\\mathbf T$ collapses to an object with zero area.\n- Also, the fact that $\\mid \\det(T) \\mid = 0$ tells us that $\\mathbf T$ has no inverse transformation. Therefore, it is impossible to recover the original triangle knowing only its image and the matrix $T$." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107160 1064 "Consider the triangle below with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7c16deb083b467c063c900196274d08c532ad4633354f375530cb2cbe270491.png)\n\nWhat is the image of this triangle under the action of the transformation  $\\mathbf{T}$ represented by the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all vertices of our triangle written as columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,0).$ Notice that these points lie on the same line. Two of them even coincide!\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the following line segment:\n\n![](images/88/88383b8eca1805c158f80d998d844d4c666b15d118a0ec26b4a1ba43c781fc76.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\nThis is an example of a linear transformation that does not preserve linear independence. \n\n* First, notice that since [math]\\det(T)= 0,[/math] the matrix [math]T[/math] is singular, which means that the vectors represented by the columns of [math] T,[/math] given below, are *not* linearly independent. \n\\[\n\\left\\{ \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\:  \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\right\\}\n\\]  \n\n- Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ and region $D,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\n \nSince $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ in this case, any object applied to $\\mathbf T$ collapses to an object with zero area.\n\n- Also, the fact that $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ tells us that $\\mathbf T$ has no inverse transformation. Therefore, it is impossible to recover the original triangle knowing only its image and the matrix $T.$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107161 1064 "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$, there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:Let $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n$det(T)|=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = 2 \\cdot 0 - 1 \\cdot 1; =-1$\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n$TX|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:Let $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n$det(T)|=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}; = 2 \\cdot 3 - 6 \\cdot 1; = 0$\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get\n$TX|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:Let $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get\n$TX|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system.![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107161 1064 "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107162 1064 "In general, for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0)$, the origin of the coordinate system." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107162 1064 "In general, for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107172 1064 "The **vector projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the portion that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends along $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{b} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nThe starting point of the vector projection is $O$, and the ending point will be the point $P$ that lies on the line containing $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b267fb009e79eb16f86be42edc2c835808b28aa3eaf1c7e857300224ee01ec.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is called the **vector projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$. It is denoted as $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}$, and it is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= OP \\cdot u \\\\\n&= comp_{b}a \\cdot u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} = \\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nis the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nFirst, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\mid a \\mid \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\, \\mid} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= comp_{b}a \\cdot u \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107172 1064 "The **vector projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the portion that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends along $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\mathbf{a}|=2,$ $|\\mathbf{b}|=3,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nThe starting point of the vector projection is $O,$ and the ending point will be the point $P$ that lies on the line containing $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b267fb009e79eb16f86be42edc2c835808b28aa3eaf1c7e857300224ee01ec.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is called the **vector projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$ It is denoted as $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and it is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =OP\\cdot u \\\\  & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta$ is the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107173 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} =\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can write an alternative formula using the formula for the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original formula for the vector projection, and also substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{\\mid \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\begin{vmatrix}b &} \\cdot \\frac{b}{\\mid b\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid b\\right|^{2}}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107173 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} =\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can write an alternative formula using the formula for the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original formula for the vector projection, and also substituting $\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}\\cdot \\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,{|}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107180 1064 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand that the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$, consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}$, and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= OP \\\\\n&= \\mid a \\mid \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= \\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid \\cos\\theta\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107180 1064 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107181 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= \\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\mid a \\mid \\mid b\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos θ \\\\ \\cos θ|= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b\\end{vmatrix}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cos θ \\\\ = & a\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b\\begin{vmatrix}} \\\\ = \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid b\\end{vmatrix}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107181 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107182 1064 "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$) is equal to $0$.\n\nFor example, consider the following illustration. Notice that, since $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ doesn't extend in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$ at all. So, the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0$.\n\n![](images/ef/efb79d8645a5df54d76c0199335aad61b258e400a98dc485ccd8b3abc8e0ba8d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals $0$ if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107182 1064 "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$) is equal to $0.$\n\nFor example, consider the following illustration. Notice that, since $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ doesn't extend in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$ at all. So, the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0.$\n\n![](images/ef/efb79d8645a5df54d76c0199335aad61b258e400a98dc485ccd8b3abc8e0ba8d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals $0$ if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107183 1064 "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, where it is known that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse.\n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\mid a \\mid \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (135^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if\n\n$$\n90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nthen $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107183 1064 "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ,$ then $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186107187 1064 "When finding the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$, we sometimes find that the resulting projection vector points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n![](images/0e/0e973e6f6484a9aad863ebfe89fc1ea6356d6a50844931306c6aa2b63be18a8a.png)\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, where it is known that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2,\\qquad \\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=3,\\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nFirst, notice that the angle between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ is obtuse. This means we expect the projection vector to point in the opposite direction to that of $\\mathbf b$.\n\nNext, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncomp_{b}a &= \\mid a \\mid \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (135^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the scalar projection is negative.\n\nThen, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{b}\\, \\mid} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= comp_{b}a \\cdot u \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b}$, as expected." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186107187 1064 "When finding the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b},$ we sometimes find that the resulting projection vector points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}.$ This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/0e/0e973e6f6484a9aad863ebfe89fc1ea6356d6a50844931306c6aa2b63be18a8a.png)\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,\\qquad |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=3,\\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nFirst, notice that the angle between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ is obtuse. This means we expect the projection vector to point in the opposite direction to that of $\\mathbf b.$\n\nNext, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the scalar projection is negative. \n\nThen, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b},$ as expected." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186111828 1064 "Just as we can have a system of linear equations, we can also have systems of linear inequalities. For example, the system\n\n![](images/42/422ff3fd1ff0704d34d5d2f098e3c524f409d2a4da7d6fa3d792020bbc8ac56d.svg)\nis solved by the points that satisfy the inequalities ![](images/23/23f2bda2195f51e1cbf95f6905d547dc9004733d89866ae9f92ef046c330417c.svg) *and* ![](images/87/877dcbf886336f3054b3df8910cee950868e2819e007cf11544a6be7f15b81d3.svg) simultaneously.\n\nTo find the points that satisfy both inequalities, we first draw the graph of the inequality ![](images/9a/9a758452dbe8e0ede75fb97296110356c18e46275000ac28ebf631d91fa05b22.svg)\n\n![](images/6f/6f3f7b6eb3109225256c5f4342333d03c19ef31b40d5ae5b24c136950a31f76a.png)\n\nNow, on top of the diagram above, we draw the graph of the second inequality, ![](images/c1/c1e37a6573ce7b419aeff619c181b43325304505c05b669394ef0818ace2555f.svg)\n\n![](images/e0/e017eefa235ac4b95009570c59fe5e5587f50bb02299305cfb9126573c0156bd.png)\n\nThe solution to the system is the *intersection* of the two regions (i.e., the part that is shaded twice).\n\n![](images/c1/c1d9b4358e15ca09e8358f8e298fc0ffa745b885adcd7ea522e07f1d221a2c90.png)\n\nThe important thing to realize is that *any* point in the shaded region above satisfies *both* inequalities." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186111828 1064 "Just as we can have a system of linear equations, we can also have systems of linear inequalities. For example, the system\n\n$$\ny \\le x + 2; y > - x\n$$\n\nis solved by the points that satisfy the inequalities\n\n$$\ny \\leq x+2\n$$\n\n*and* $y>-x$ simultaneously.\n\nTo find the points that satisfy both inequalities, we first draw the graph of the inequality\n\n$$\ny \\leq x + 2{:}\n$$\n\n![](images/f9/f96de3a7a91d0bfef399dd4c5810b9c7d0388137841dff98cd4fddb954664d3a.png)\n\nNow, on top of the diagram above, we draw the graph of the second inequality, $y > -x{:}$\n\n![](images/ac/ac101ee6e29e6caac9ecad1171a14c59063e48d8a04dd81476a68308f855e517.png)\n\nThe solution to the system is the *intersection* of the two regions (i.e., the part that is shaded twice).\n\n![](images/26/26aac146d9abd3e566c9534e2e11584fb5b02dd4fed06c1b49af545d7a1ba93e.png)\n\nThe important thing to realize is that *any* point in the shaded region above satisfies *both* inequalities." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186111866 1064 "Let's recall the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}x+{sin}^{2}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we divide both sides of this identity by ${\\color{black}\\cos^2 x},$ we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos^2 x} {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} + \\dfrac {\\sin^2 x}{\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\t = \\dfrac 1 {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n1+ \\left(\\dfrac {\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac 1 {\\cos x}\\right)^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identities into $(\\ast)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}^{2} \\\\ 1+(\\operatorname{tan}x)^{2} & =(\\operatorname{sec}x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x  = \\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nThe final identity is called the **secant-tangent identity**. We often use it to simplify trigonometric expressions containing both tangent and secant functions." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186111866 1064 "Let's recall the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x = 1\n$$\n\nIf we divide both sides of this identity by ${\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}$, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos^2 x} {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} + \\dfrac {\\sin^2 x}{\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} = \\dfrac 1 {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n1+ \\left(\\dfrac {\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac 1 {\\cos x}\\right)^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identities into $(\\ast)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + (\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{\\cos x})^{2} \\\\\n1 + (\\tan x)^{2} &= (\\sec x)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nThe final identity is called the **secant-tangent identity**. We often use it to simplify trigonometric expressions containing both tangent and secant functions." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186111871 1064 "When simplifying expressions containing $\\cot$ and $\\csc$, we can use the following **cotangent-cosecant identity**:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nThe identity above can be obtained by starting with the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^{2} x + \\cos^{2} x = 1\n$$\n\nWe divide both sides by $\\color{black}\\sin^2 x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^{2} x}{\\sin^{2} x} + \\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{\\sin^{2} x} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above using\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x = \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186111871 1064 "When simplifying expressions containing $\\cot$ and $\\csc,$ we can use the following **cotangent-cosecant identity**:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nThe identity above can be obtained by starting with the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe divide both sides by $\\color{black}\\sin^2 x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}x}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}x} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above using $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$ and $\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}$ gives\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x  = \\csc^2 x,\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186111872 1064 "If we subtract $1$ from both sides of the cotangent-cosecant identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nThis is an alternate form of the cotangent-cosecant identity.\n\nWe can also get a second alternate form if we start with\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand subtract $\\cot^2\\theta$ from both sides. This gives\n\n$$\n\\csc^2\\theta - \\cot^2\\theta = 1\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 false] [17592186111872 1064 "We can rewrite the cotangent-cosecant identity in other useful forms.\n\nStarting with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $1$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nThis is one alternate form of the cotangent-cosecant identity.\n\nWe can also rewrite the identity another way. Starting again with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $\\cot^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\csc^2\\theta - \\cot^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc^2 \\theta - 1}.$\n\nFirst, we recognize that the denominator matches one of the identities above.\n\nSubstituting the identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{csc}^{2}\\theta -1} & =\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}\\theta }={tan}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533436 17592186045585 true] [17592186111876 1064 "How do we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nAs usual, we can use a table of known values.\n\nIn order to determine these known values, let's first look at the graph of the sine function.\n\n![](images/92/92dd5df32efd0c8413d7d7890cc21e8d4e76ad803491f0645b1c4ba98356055e.png)\n\nThe sine function is a one-to-one function on the interval\n\n$$\nx\\in\\left[-\\dfrac \\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right]\n$$\n\nSo if we restrict the domain to this interval, we can define the inverse function\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\nRemember that the inverse swaps the domain and range of the original function. So the domain of $y=\\arcsin x$ is $x\\in[-1,1]$, while the range is\n\n$$\ny \\in\\left[-\\dfrac \\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right]\n$$\n\nIn the domain, we create a table of values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\arcsin x$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ |\n\nUsing this table, we can now plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arcsin x\n$$\n\n![](images/bd/bdf5638fb2a758a9cb268d7255720ec0c2b099663d118590f08949c5c7016b81.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1]$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}2, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right]$.\n- The 